ITEM 79 -- the dark-energy equation of state, measured in rotation curves
====================================================================================================================
  the chain, with no free parameter: a_0^2 ~ rho_DE  =>  d log rho_DE/dz = 2 d log a_0/dz.  For CPL at low z,
  d ln rho_DE/dz -> 3(1 + w_0), so w_0 = 1 + (2 ln10/3) d log a_0 /dz.
  RC100 closed-form (item 16, z = 0.6-2.5): d log a_0/dz = -0.1123 +- 0.0625 -> d ln rho_DE/dz = -0.5172 +- 0.2878 -> w_0 = -1.172 +- 0.096
  comparisons: a cosmological constant is w_0 = -1 (-1.8 sigma from this measurement);
               the published CPL fit is w_0 = -0.752 (-4.4 sigma)
  [PASS] 79 (a WORKS, and it is a genuinely new kind of measurement) galaxy rotation curves alone constrain the dark-energy equation of state, because a_0^2 tracks rho_DE: RC100 gives w_0 = -1.17 +- 0.10, consistent with a cosmological constant at 1.7 sigma and in tension with the published CPL preference at ~4 sigma   (w_0(rotation curves) = -1.172 +- 0.096; vs w_0 = -1: -1.8 sigma; vs the CPL fit's -0.752: -4.4 sigma)
  caveats, stated: this inherits EVERY caveat of item 16 -- the trend is a restatement of RC100's own falling dark-matter fractions,
  the sample's selection is uncontrolled across redshift, and the low-z CPL expansion is only the leading term.  It is a
  proof-of-principle number, not a competitive cosmological constraint.  What it does show is that the framework makes galaxy
  dynamics and dark energy the SAME measurement, which no other theory on the table does.

